nice to see you it's been a few  a little while since sundance yes yes  um so i have a million questions and i  have six minutes so let's just get  started  um do you think do you think the movie  if it not been for covet obviously there  you never know if this movie would even  exist but if you had the opportunity  to film on location do you think you  would have done anything like  adding a location to this film or did  you always envision it  um as one location um look i think the  the the restrictions that kovid uh  required was um  uh is what made this film  possible so just in the sense that you  know  i wanted it to be a sort of we needed it  to be as safe as possible so as bare  bones as possible two actors one house  one location  essentially one long scene no real  special effects props  location moves no bells and whistles  um one camera a few lights  that's it um not extensive you know art  direction  so you know that's kind of what was  exciting about it is  how how can i you know it was sort of  born out of this exercise of can we  tell us a story that's engaging  and uh emotional and moving and  cinematic  but with you know the very few resources  you did the roger deakins of just one  camera one one shot no coverage  yeah we tend to i mean i tend to shoot  euphoria that way too i mean i would say  80  some odd percent of euphoria is just  single camera um  as well but uh yeah i just i prefer one  camera only because  then you're not having to compromise  lighting wise and  and movement wise so uh how many days  did you actually  allow yourself to shoot this movie uh  we shot for 14 days but uh  funnily enough the first day of shooting  i kind of had the uh marcel rev and i  cinematographer who i always collaborate  with um we had a  very kind of you know uh you know uh  sort of formalist idea of how we're  gonna shoot it is gonna be like a  premanger film  all dolly work very old school so  where should we shoot the first day  we're looking at it we get into the car  i say great work the actors we shoot  about you know 15 pages get into the car  i said this isn't this looks like a  whiskey commercial we gotta scrap it i  can't you know and he's like i  completely agree  it's not messy enough we need more life  in it we go to sleep we wake up the next  morning we go to set  and we we decided we're gonna shoot the  whole thing handheld it's gonna be like  bullet ballet or something you know like  messy handheld black and white tell the  actors great we shoot all day i look  up marcel he's got a thousand foot mag  on his shoulder he's like covered in  sweat and i just kind of shake my head  and he shakes his head  and we know in that instant that we're  literally gonna throw that day out too  the only problem is i don't want to tell  the actors because i don't want them to  be like are you kidding we just didn't  done 80 takes of the first 15 minutes  and we're gonna throw it out so i'm like  great work let's go to sleep  come back tomorrow and only on the third  day when we were setting out for that  exterior shot did i think we did the  whole film click and that's when we  realized  okay this is the movie we need to start  objectively  we need to look at it as a play in some  senses because  the structure of it is very specific  there is no act breaks there's no  place that it's it's heading it's just a  sort of socratic  dialogue uh you know where these two  characters are just  taking shots at one another and that's  when we discovered  kind of how we were gonna shoot it and  then we needed a couple extra days and  that's what got us to 14 days total  including first of all it's an amazing  story that i wish i could dig deep on  but i don't have time did you guys ever  consider  at all going to a studio and saying  would you like to pay for this would you  like to finance  because i believe you you guys did it on  your own  yeah no we never we we never wanted to  do that only because we wanted to  first of all we wanted to create a  financial structure um for the movie  that um  uh where we could uh where we could give  our crew  actual back end in the in the film so  that in  in in success they were able to kind of  you know share  in the benefits and also we wanted to  carve out a portion of the back end for  feeding america and donate it um uh to  charity so we wanted full control over  in that sense and also we didn't want to  have to answer to anybody  so like as i was writing you know my  producer kevin turner called me he was  like sam you better finish this script  because i just put 80 000  worth of black and white film on my  credit card and  my wife is saying i just booked some  hotels z's putting in money and it's  just  we just kept it going that way um and uh  because i don't think we would have been  able to we wouldn't have been able to  to to come up with this structure which  i think is  a structure that's important in terms of  filmmaking i have never understood why  julio perez the editor that i work with  marcel rep  i don't understand why they don't have  ownership  in in the films that we make together  they are as  important as any other person on that  set  and we wanted to reflect that um  and and have uh uh something to point to  in the future  i gotta stop i'm just gonna say congrats  on the movie next time we'll do a longer  conversation please  i would love that thank you

